//
// @PLN153 phase 1 — the REFUSE half of @FR-N-Store's pair: an undischarged τ? into a DECLARED τ local is reported (a runtime index, so no in-bounds proof elides the null).
//
// The pair exists so the citation at the rule's home is a receipt for a measured
// refusal, not a comment: `rule_tags.py check` counts the citation, this file is what
// makes it honest.  Its expectation is the diagnostic's own text, pinned as a substring.
//
// The severity is the rule's: a WARNING at full width — the store PROCEEDS and the slot
// holds the null (`(N-Store)`: "REJECTED — a WARNING for most τ"), an ERROR only at a narrow
// width (`153-n-store-refused-into-a-narrow-local`).  Phase 3b: the declared local used to
// be a hard error at every width, the one place the code was stricter than the rule.
//
// @falsified-at: 8498fdf1 → here (hand-measured, a warning-only channel): the parent build
//   REFUSES this program (`cannot change type from integer to integer?`), so the warning
//   below is unmatched and the file fails there; on this build the store proceeds and `f`
//   answers null.
//   HOLDS: on --interpret, assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on
//   both trees, measured by `scripts/falsify.sh --bulk` against 8498fdf1; falsify prints only
//   the channels that MOVE, and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both.
//   The exit channel carries this one on that backend. The native side was not scored in that
//   run.
// @EXPECT_WARNING: a nullable `integer?` is stored into the local `x` of the non-null type `integer`
fn f(v: vector<integer>, i: integer) -> integer { x: integer = v[i]; return x; }
fn main() {
  assert(f([1, 2], 5) == null, "the slot holds the null: {f([1, 2], 5)}");
  assert(f([1, 2], 1) == 2, "an in-range read is the value: {f([1, 2], 1)}");
  println("declared local reported");
}
